From 631ff2e3a40a8dd872b7f6ed4819fd831b5e2ea2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 04:36:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] feat: a home that answers one question first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The home says one sentence: who you are here, whether anything depends on
you, where we are, what the journey looks like. Four blocks, all of them
with an origin in the database.

The opening greets the person, using the name that has existed on the
profile since phase 3 and was never used here — the screen used to greet
the brand. It is also much shorter: the full-bleed slab used to eat the
whole first screen on a phone, so the client had to scroll to find out
whether anything was waiting for them. That slab moved to where colour
means something, which is the attention block.

The journey on the home is three stages — previous, current, next — and the
full one stays on the project page. Eight blocks are a map, not an answer,
and having both screens draw the same map is what made the portal feel like
it had two homes. The window walks by position, so a skipped stage still
appears with its label: hiding a stage the agency chose to skip would be
editing the project's history on the client's screen.

When there is no current stage the block says what is true — the cycle is
finished, the project has not started, nothing is in progress — and when
the journey is empty it disappears. Nothing describes activity the database
does not assert.

The stage sentence is printed once. In the calm state the attention block
already carries it, so the now block does not repeat it; that decision
lives in the composition, not inside the components.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
